{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Users\\\\23201\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Microsoft\\\\WindowsApps\\\\pwsh.exe\" -NoProfile -Command \"Get-Content -LiteralPath 'E:\\\\desktop\\\\MATLAB\\\\artifacts\\\\phase58\\\\native\\\\fixtures\\\\task_A\\\\.agents\\\\skills\\\\matlab-orchestrator\\\\SKILL.md','E:\\\\desktop\\\\MATLAB\\\\artifacts\\\\phase58\\\\native\\\\fixtures\\\\task_A\\\\.agents\\\\skills\\\\matlab-runner\\\\SKILL.md' -Raw\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: matlab-orchestrator\ndescription: MATLAB agentic workflow router. Use this whenever the user asks for MATLAB, Simulink, .m/.mlx/.slx work, toolbox-driven engineering, paper reproduction, simulation, code generation, data analysis, control, robotics, signal/image/AI workflows, or wants an end-to-end automated MATLAB task with validation. For Simulink tasks on this machine, default to a visible workflow: open MATLAB/Simulink windows first so the user can watch modeling, simulation, tuning, and plotting progress.\n---\n\n# MATLAB Orchestrator\n\nUse this skill as the entry point for MATLAB work. The goal is to turn a loose engineering request into a closed-loop workflow: select the right specialist skill, run MATLAB when possible, save artifacts, and verify the result before reporting back.\n\n## Execution Requirement On This Machine\n\nOn this machine, MATLAB and Simulink work should default to the official **MATLAB MCP auto** workflow, not a fresh `matlab -batch` session. Treat the visible MATLAB desktop as the primary and expected execution path for engineering tasks, especially Simulink modeling, iterative control tuning, and repeated read-modify-simulate loops.\n\nUse this priority order:\n\n1. Official MATLAB MCP in `auto` mode, reusing an existing shared MATLAB session when present.\n2. Official MATLAB MCP in `existing` / `new` mode only if the user explicitly requests those modes.\n3. `matlab -batch` only if the user explicitly asks for batch, CI, or headless execution.\n\n## First pass\n\n1. Identify the task family.\n2. Check whether an official MATLAB MCP auto configuration is available first.\n3. If the task requires MATLAB work on this machine, stay on MCP unless the user explicitly requests a headless/batch path.\n4. Inspect installed toolboxes with `ver` before choosing toolbox-specific APIs.\n5. Prefer official MathWorks documentation and installed examples over memory.\n6. Treat forum posts and blogs as hints only; verify every API and parameter locally.\n\n## Local Simulink Visualization Preference\n\nThe user prefers to see Simulink work happen live, not only receive final files. For Simulink modeling, simulation, control tuning, or code generation tasks:\n\n1. Start by opening a visible MATLAB/Simulink session and the relevant `.slx` model with `load_system` / `open_system`.\n2. If creating a new model, create and save the model early, then open it before continuing with automated block edits.\n3. Use `open_system(modelName)`, `set_param(modelName,\"ZoomFactor\",\"FitSystem\")`, scopes, figures, and live plots so the user can watch the process.\n4. Prefer a visible demonstration step before or alongside batch validation. Batch logs and exported artifacts are still required for acceptance, but they should not replace the visible model/plot experience.\n5. When an already-open MATLAB window is present, try to execute `open_system` in that visible session or bring the Simulink model window to the front. Confirm the expected model window title in the final report.\n\n## MCP-Specific Guidance\n\nWhen an official MATLAB MCP auto path is available:\n\n1. Treat the visible MATLAB desktop session as the primary control plane.\n2. Prefer MCP-driven model edits, simulation commands, workspace inspection, and iterative tuning in that same session.\n3. Do not silently switch to batch mode just because batch would be easier.\n4. In the final report, explicitly say that the task ran through MCP, and note any remaining blocker if a required MCP action could not be completed.\n\n## Readiness Gate Before Any MCP Run\n\nNever infer MCP readiness from a config file, a recorded `pid`, or a listening port. Run the staged probe first and read the per-stage verdict:\n\n```powershell\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\scripts\\Test-MatlabMcpExistingSession.ps1 -Json\n```\n\nThe probe grades install, session config, client registration, protocol handshake, read-only execution, and file read-back as **separate checks**. Consequences you must respect when routing work:\n\n- If `mcp.protocol-handshake` is `FAIL`, do not attempt MCP edits; report the exact blocker (HTTP status plus JSON-RPC error) and ask before switching to batch.\n- If `mcp.readonly-exec` is `SKIP` because the server exposes no evaluation tool, say so �� a successful handshake only proves the protocol layer, not that MATLAB can run anything.\n- If the session file is stale (missing certificate, dead pid), the honest report is \"existing session is stale\", not \"MCP is configured\".\n\n## Closed-loop Definition\n\nA MATLAB task is complete only when it has:\n\n- A runnable command or script.\n- Logs captured from MATLAB.\n- Artifacts saved in a predictable folder.\n- A numerical, visual, or structural acceptance check.\n- A short explanation of what was verified and what remains unverified.\n\nThe acceptance verdict must come from the unified report, not from your impression:\n\n- Report file: `artifacts/validation/acceptance_report.json`, schema `schemas/acceptance-report.schema.json`.\n- `overallStatus` is `PASS` only when every check passed. Any `SKIP` makes it `SKIP` (exit code `3`), and `SKIP` is never converted into a pass.\n- `achievedLevel` is the highest **contiguous** level reached: if L2 was skipped, a passing L3 still only yields L1.\n- Route the work so that at least the level the user asked for is genuinely executed. If it cannot be executed here (no license, no toolbox, no board), say that explicitly instead of producing an L1 artifact and calling it done.\n\nLevel routing hints:\n\n| If the user asks for | The minimum honest evidence is |\n| --- | --- |\n| \"generate code\" | L2: real compile artifact (library/executable), not just `.c` files |\n| \"make the model work\" | L3: simulation reaches stop time and key signals match expectations |\n| \"make it robust\" | L4: parameter sweep plus failure-mode checks (NaN, illegal inputs) |\n| \"deploy to the board\" | L5: download plus read-back �� generic C codegen is not deployment |\n\nFailure classification and retry policy live in `matlab-runner`; classify before retrying, and never auto-retry destructive or non-idempotent operations.\n\n## Routing\n\nDo not choose a skill from memory. Run the matcher, which reads the weighted term\ntable in `assets/routing.json`:\n\n```matlab\nr = mas_route(\"������������������ THD\");\nr.schemaVersion   % '1.1'\nr.decision        % 'ROUTE' | 'CLARIFY' | 'AMBIGUOUS' | 'BLOCKED'\nr.loadOrder       % {'matlab-runner','matlab-simulink-modeling','matlab-signal-vision-ai'}\nr.nextAction      % 'load' | 'ask' | 'stop'\n```\n\nConsume the decision before anything else, and do not override it:\n\n- `ROUTE` �� load `loadOrder` in order. It is already dependency-sorted;\n  `matlab-runner` is always first because every route needs execution.\n- `CLARIFY` �� the best score is below `minScore`. Ask one question; never use\n  `ranked(1)` as if it were a decision.\n- `AMBIGUOUS` �� two domains tied. Ask which one carries the core work.\n- `BLOCKED` �� stop. `missing` names the absent skill, damaged `SKILL.md`,\n  unlicensed hard toolbox, missing dependency, or dependency cycle. Do not\n  substitute a different domain and do not run a partial workflow; `loadOrder`\n  is empty by contract.\n\nSecondary skills join when their score is at least `multiSkillRatio * top`.\n`matlab-testing-ci` joins only when the task is meant to be reusable or\nexplicitly needs acceptance/regression. A soft requirement that is unlicensed\nappears in `notes` and does not block.\n\nThe router is lexical. Negation is handled per clause, and ASCII terms of three\ncharacters or fewer match on word boundaries, but a prompt with no matching\nterms must come back as `CLARIFY` rather than a guess. The full field list,\nblocker kinds, and consumer rules are in\n[`references/routing-contract.md`](references/routing-contract.md).\n\n| Skill | Domain |\n| --- | --- |\n| `matlab-runner` | execution, logs, prechecks, artifacts (always loaded) |\n| `matlab-simulink-modeling` | `.slx`, Stateflow, Simscape, simulation, sweeps |\n| `matlab-signal-vision-ai` | signals, filters, spectra, images, video, AI |\n| `matlab-control-optimization` | controllers, state space, identification, MPC, optimization |\n| `matlab-data-analysis` | tables, statistics, fitting, figures, reports |\n| `matlab-codegen-deploy` | C/C++/CUDA/HDL generation, MEX, embedded deployment |\n| `matlab-robotics-autonomy` | ROS, kinematics, planning, navigation, sensor fusion |\n| `matlab-testing-ci` | unit tests, Simulink Test, coverage, CI, acceptance |\n\n`tests/test_routing.m` 9/9 preserves the Phase 4 brief examples and the two\n\"must not guess\" cases. `tests/test_routing_contract.m` 16/16 pins the\nversioned contract, including refusal, dependency order, and availability\nblockers. Routing changes are covered by both.\n\n## Default Artifact Layout\n\nUse this layout for generated work unless the project already has a convention:\n\n```text\nartifacts/\n  logs/\n  figures/\n  data/\n  models/\n  reports/\n  validation/\n```\n\nFor paper reproduction, add:\n\n```text\nrepro/\n  source/\n  scripts/\n  figures/\n  results/\n  manifest.json\n```\n\n## Source Discipline\n\nUse sources in this order:\n\n1. Local MATLAB `help`, `doc`, `ver`, `which`, `exist`, `license`.\n2. MathWorks official documentation and release notes.\n3. MathWorks GitHub repositories and MATLAB Actions.\n4. Peer-reviewed paper, official dataset, or author repository.\n5. GitHub issues, MathWorks Answers, Zhihu, blogs, and forums as untrusted heuristics.\n\nNever cite a forum workaround as fact until a local MATLAB command confirms it.\n\n## Reporting\n\nKeep the final answer concise and practical, and keep the three categories separate:\n\n1. **Verified**: command, exit code, and the acceptance report path.\n2. **Executed but failed or skipped**: the `reason` field, verbatim.\n3. **Not executed**: state it plainly and name the missing prerequisite (license, toolbox, hardware, network).\n\nNever present a skipped check as a supported capability, and never present a generated artifact as a successful build.\n\r\n---\nname: matlab-runner\ndescription: Run MATLAB tasks from Codex with reliable MCP-based execution, logs, artifacts, toolbox inventory, smoke tests, and reproducible validation. Use whenever MATLAB code, .m/.mlx scripts, MATLAB tests, local toolbox probing, or command-line automation is needed.\n---\n\n# MATLAB Runner\n\nUse this skill to execute MATLAB work safely and repeatably through the official MATLAB MCP server, preferably in `auto` mode, and only use terminal batch workflows when the user explicitly asks for them. It is the foundation skill for all other MATLAB skills.\n\n## References �� load on demand\n\nThis file carries the workflow. The tables below are **not** loaded unless the task\nactually reaches them, because each is a few hundred tokens of detail that costs\ncontext on every run and goes stale on every MATLAB release.\n\n| Load this | when |\n|---|---|\n| `references/toolbox-feature-map.md` | resolving a product name to a licence feature |\n| `references/mcp-readiness-ladder.md` | about to claim MCP is ready, or MCP failed |\n| `references/acceptance-levels.md` | about to write an acceptance report |\n| `references/failure-classes.md` | a run failed and you are deciding whether to retry |\n\n## Execution Rules\n\n1. Prefer the official MATLAB MCP auto mode for MATLAB work on this machine.\n2. Do not silently fall back to `matlab -batch` when MCP is expected.\n3. Capture logs, version info, working directory, and artifact paths where applicable.\n4. Run from a project root, not from a random temporary folder, unless isolation is required.\n5. Put generated files under `artifacts/` or a user-specified output folder.\n6. Keep scripts deterministic: seed random generators and record data sources.\n\n## Precheck Before A Long Run\n\nPhase 3B.1 shipped five scripts and none ran on the first attempt; most of those\nfailures were detectable in seconds. Run the cheap checks first, in this order:\n\n```matlab\nr = mas_precheck('function', 'boost_params');                 % exists?\nr = mas_precheck('struct',   cfg, {'Vin','L','C'});           % fields present?\nr = mas_precheck('numeric',  x, struct('finite',true));        % NaN/Inf?\nr = mas_precheck('index',    mask, numel(target));             % mask length\nr = mas_precheck('toolbox',  'Control System Toolbox');        % product vs feature\nr = mas_precheck('model',    'matlab/boost/models/boost_avg.slx');\n```\n\nThe `index` check exists because MATLAB does **not** error when a logical mask is\nshorter than the array it indexes: it silently returns the first `numel(mask)`\nelements. In Phase 3B.2 that turned every `iLref` reading into `0` and nearly\nproduced the conclusion \"the current command is railed\". A length check makes it\nloud. `tests/test_precheck.m` covers positive and must-fail cases.\n\nNever start a 20-minute matrix after a failed precheck; fix the precheck first.\n\n## MCP Required By Default\n\nA config file never proves MCP works, and `sessionDetails.json`, a listening `pid`\nor a client entry in `.mcp.json` all look identical to a file-existence check.\nBefore claiming readiness, run the staged probe and read its verdict:\n\n```powershell\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\scripts\\Test-MatlabMcpExistingSession.ps1 -Json\n```\n\nThe seven-stage ladder, the rules that must not be relaxed (only stage 4 upgrades a\nlicence SKIP to PASS; a failed stage makes every later stage SKIP), and the\nmachine-specific decision rule are in `references/mcp-readiness-ladder.md`. Load it\nwhen you are about to make a readiness claim �� not before.\n\nIf MCP is unavailable, **report the blocker** instead of silently changing execution\nmode. A batch rerun that succeeds hides the problem the user needs to see.\n\n## Preferred Wrapper\n\nIf the user explicitly requests batch execution in this repository, prefer:\n\n```powershell\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\scripts\\Invoke-MatlabBatch.ps1 -Script .\\path\\to\\script.m -TimeoutSeconds 1800\n```\n\n`-TimeoutSeconds` is mandatory in spirit: a batch run without a timeout can hang forever. The wrapper exits `124` on timeout, `2` when MATLAB cannot be found, and otherwise returns the real MATLAB exit code. A timed-out run terminates the whole process tree and still keeps the partial logs.\n\nFor official MCP auto-mode setup and validation in this repository, prefer:\n\n```powershell\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\scripts\\Setup-MatlabMcpExistingSession.ps1\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\scripts\\Test-MatlabMcpExistingSession.ps1\n```\n\nFor a full local smoke test:\n\n```powershell\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\\scripts\\Test-MatlabSkills.ps1\n```\n\nIf the wrapper is not available, call MATLAB directly:\n\n```powershell\nmatlab -batch \"disp(version); v=ver; disp({v.Name}')\"\n```\n\n## MATLAB Discovery\n\nUse this order to find MATLAB:\n\n1. Official MATLAB MCP auto configuration\n2. Existing official MATLAB shared session metadata when present\n3. `$env:MATLAB_EXE`, `Get-Command matlab`, and known install paths only for setup/verification of the MCP environment\n\nVerify before running expensive work:\n\n```matlab\ndisp(version)\nv = ver;\n{v.Name}'\n```\n\n## Error Handling\n\nWhen MATLAB fails:\n\n- Read the first error and the deepest stack frame.\n- Check a missing toolbox with `mas_precheck('toolbox', X)` rather than by hand.\n  `license('test', ...)` takes a **feature** name, not the product display name, and\n  the two differ often enough to burn a run: on R2026a\n  `license('test','Deep_Learning_Toolbox')` returns `0` while\n  `license('test','Neural_Network_Toolbox')` returns `1` for the same installed\n  product. The verified product��feature map, with its provenance, is in\n  `references/toolbox-feature-map.md`.\n\n  ```matlab\n  r = mas_precheck('toolbox', 'Deep Learning Toolbox');\n  r.pass, r.detail     % 1, \"installed 1, feature Neural_Network_Toolbox licensed 1\"\n  ```\n\n  A product missing from the map returns a failure that says so, rather than\n  guessing. (An earlier revision of this file told the agent to call\n  `masacc.availability`. No such package exists in this repository or in any\n  installed toolbox �� a call to it fails immediately. That instruction is removed,\n  and `mas_capability_check` now fails the build if any file references it again.)\n- Check path ambiguity with `which functionName -all`.\n- Re-run a minimal reproduction before editing large code.\n- Save the failing command and log path.\n- If the failure was on MCP, report the exact MCP blocker and stop hiding it behind a batch rerun.\n\n## Acceptance Checks\n\nReport which level was actually proven, not a vague \"it works\". The levels are\ncumulative and **never upgrade themselves**: producing a file is an L1 claim, and\ncalling that \"deployed\" is the most common overstatement here because the file\nreally is there.\n\n| Level | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| L1 | artifact produced |\n| L2 | build/compile succeeded |\n| L3 | numeric result matches a golden value |\n| L4 | multi-scenario and failure modes |\n| L5 | real hardware run, read back |\n\n`SKIP` means \"not executed\" and is not a pass; a report containing any SKIP has\n`overallStatus = SKIP` and exit code `3`. `FAIL` or `SKIP` without a `reason` is\nrejected at construction time. Which single checks are worth running per situation\nis in `references/acceptance-levels.md` �� load it before writing the report, not\nbefore every command.\n\n## Failure Classification\n\nClassify before retrying. Retrying a `LICENSE_ERROR` or an `ENVIRONMENT_ERROR` only\nwastes time, and a retried `VALIDATION_ERROR` can turn a clear numeric mismatch into\na vague \"flaky\". The eight classes, which are auto-retryable, and the cap of 5\nattempts are in `references/failure-classes.md`. The rule that matters most:\n**non-idempotent or destructive operations (downloads, installs, writes) are never\nretried without explicit user approval**, because the retry is the part that causes\ndamage.\n\n## Paper Reproduction Mode\n\nFor open-source paper projects:\n\n1. Record paper URL, commit hash, dataset URL, MATLAB release, toolbox list.\n2. Run the smallest official example first.\n3. Reproduce one figure or table before scaling to the whole paper.\n4. Save `manifest.json`, raw logs, processed data, and generated figures.\n5. Mark differences from the paper explicitly instead of smoothing them over.\n\n## One Dependency Mechanism, Five Failure Classes\n\n`mas_precheck('toolbox', X)` and `mas_dep_probe` now share a single product table\n(`mas_product_map.m`). Before this there were two copies and they could drift; the\nold one lived inside `mas_precheck` and the new one inside the probe.\n\n```matlab\nd = mas_dep_probe('product', 'Deep Learning Toolbox');\nd.state    % OK | NOT_INSTALLED | NOT_LICENSED | UNKNOWN_PRODUCT | EXEC_FAILED\nd.level    % 1 function resolved, 3 product installed, 4 feature licensed, 5 probe ran\n```\n\n`NOT_INSTALLED`, `NOT_LICENSED` and `UNKNOWN_PRODUCT` are different problems with\ndifferent fixes, so they are different states. The probe reports which level it\nreached, so \"it does not work\" becomes a specific sentence.\n\nHistorical product names are aliases of the current row (`Neural Network Toolbox` ->\n`Deep Learning Toolbox`, `Statistics Toolbox` -> `Statistics and Machine Learning\nToolbox`), because a user typing the old name must not get \"not licensed\". Covered\nby `tests/test_dep_probe.m`, including must-fail dependency cases.\n\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
